########################################################################################################################
# Help for processes
########################################################################################################################
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
ik : interactive kill. Opens one fzf picker listing every process with its PID, listening
     ports, user and command line — so a single query matches either a name or a port, with
     no mode to choose first:

       PID     PORTS           USER       COMMAND
       1234    :8080,:9090     user       java -jar myapp.jar
       5678    -               user       node worker.js

     Type 8080 to find who holds the port, or java to find it by name. TAB marks several
     processes, ENTER validates. The marked processes are listed back and confirmed before
     any kill -9. Your own shell is never listed.
     Port detection uses `ss` (iproute2) if available, falls back to `netstat` (net-tools);
     with neither, the ports column shows "-" and the picker still works by command name.

ik PATTERN : same picker, opened pre-filtered on PATTERN.
ik --pattern VALUE : skip the picker, use the historical numbered kill-by-pattern flow.
ik --port VALUE : skip the picker, kill whatever listens on VALUE.

     Without fzf — or with NIXLPER_KILL_FUZZY=false — `ik` falls back to the historical
     prompt asking for a kill mode (port/pattern) then a value.

  Settings (nconf):
    NIXLPER_KILL_FUZZY   bool   true   Use the fzf unified picker when fzf is installed

pc PORT : quick-check what process is listening on PORT (process name, PID, command line,
     suggested action). Read-only — does not kill anything. Uses the same port detection as `ik`.
     Configurable via nconf: NIXLPER_PORT_CHECK_SHOW_CMDLINE (bool, default true).
